WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:00.638
Panes, tabs, the path bar, the filter, the status line, the toolbar and Help.

2
00:00:00.638 --> 00:00:03.250
Two panes. Each pane has its own tabs, folder, selection and sort order.

3
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:05.859
Tabs. Every pane keeps its own tabs. The graduation cap marks the tutorial's tabs.

4
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:08.688
Path bar and filter. Click a folder in the path to go there; type to filter the list.

5
00:00:08.688 --> 00:00:11.295
Status line. What is selected, how many bytes, and how long the folder took to read.

6
00:00:11.295 --> 00:00:13.739
The focused pane. Keys go to the pane with the accent line. Switch Pane moves the focus.

7
00:00:13.739 --> 00:00:16.565
Toolbar and menus. Every command is in the menu bar with its key, and in the command palette.

8
00:00:16.565 --> 00:00:19.595
Help is always here. The graduation cap, or Help in the palette, explains any feature again.

9
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:20.738
Let us explore how to move through folders with the keyboard and the path bar.

10
00:00:20.738 --> 00:00:22.017
Move the cursor to a folder and press Enter to open it. You can also open folders with a double click.

11
00:00:22.017 --> 00:00:22.647
Go to the enclosing folder to move up one level. The cursor returns to the folder you just left.

12
00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:25.057
The path bar shows where you are. Click any folder in the path to jump directly to that level.

13
00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:25.688
Keep your current pane in place and open the folder under the cursor in the opposite pane.

14
00:00:25.688 --> 00:00:26.321
The same command works in either direction, so you can arrange a source and destination quickly.

15
00:00:26.321 --> 00:00:26.949
File Commander watches folders for changes. Refresh also reads the directory again when you need it.

16
00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:28.345
Page Up and Page Down move through a long list a screen at a time. Home and End jump to its beginning and end.

17
00:00:28.345 --> 00:00:43.846
Hold an arrow key to move continuously. The list scrolls to keep the cursor in view.

18
00:00:43.846 --> 00:00:46.578
Open a zip archive just like a folder to browse its contents. The archive lesson covers extraction and creation.

19
00:00:47.161 --> 00:00:47.725
Type to show only matching names; Backspace edits; a new folder clears the filter.

20
00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:50.536
Type to filter. Typing in a list shows only the names containing what you type. Case is ignored.

21
00:00:50.537 --> 00:00:54.510
Backspace edits it.

22
00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.747
Some keys are commands. Keys bound to commands, such as Space, + and ?, run them instead. Rebind them to filter with them.

23
00:00:57.748 --> 00:00:58.397
A new folder starts unfiltered.

24
00:00:58.951 --> 00:00:59.514
Mark items with Space, by pattern, or all at once; commands act on the selection.

25
00:00:59.514 --> 00:01:02.884
Mark items. Select or Deselect marks the item under the cursor and moves down.

26
00:01:02.884 --> 00:01:03.512
Deselect All clears it.

27
00:01:03.512 --> 00:01:04.868
Select by pattern. Every name that matches *.mov. Case is ignored.

28
00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:06.842
Deselect by pattern. Takes matches out of the selection again.

29
00:01:06.842 --> 00:01:09.604
Select All respects the filter. With a filter, Select All marks only what you see.

30
00:01:09.604 --> 00:01:12.626
Commands act on the selection. With nothing selected, they act on the item under the cursor.

31
00:01:12.626 --> 00:01:15.161
Prefer Finder-style selection?. Settings › General › Selection style: arrow keys select, Command -click adds.

32
00:01:15.738 --> 00:01:16.306
Open, switch, reorder and close tabs; a tab can show a folder, a server or an archive.

33
00:01:16.306 --> 00:01:18.605
New tab. New Tab opens a tab beside the current one, in the focused pane.

34
00:01:18.605 --> 00:01:20.098
Switch tabs. Previous Tab and Next Tab cycle through them; a click works too.

35
00:01:20.098 --> 00:01:20.745
Close a tab.

36
00:01:20.745 --> 00:01:23.796
Any kind of place. A tab shows a local folder, an S S H server or an archive. Drag a tab to reorder it.

37
00:01:24.351 --> 00:01:24.919
Sort by name, size or date, show more columns, use one pane, and resize the panes.

38
00:01:24.919 --> 00:01:28.129
Sort by size. Click a column header, or use View › Sort By. Again reverses the order.

39
00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:30.071
Or by date.

40
00:01:30.072 --> 00:01:32.981
More columns. Show permissions and owner:group, or right-click a column header.

41
00:01:32.981 --> 00:01:34.940
One pane, full width. Single Pane shows only the focused pane. Again brings both back.

42
00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.568
The other pane is still the destination. The bar above the path says where copies and moves go.

43
00:01:37.568 --> 00:01:40.040
Switch Pane shows it. Still one pane, full width.

44
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.664
Resize the panes. Drag the line between them.

45
00:01:43.215 --> 00:01:43.781
We will copy photos to a backup folder while continuing to use the file manager.

46
00:01:43.781 --> 00:01:47.136
Select the photos you want to copy. The folder in the other pane becomes the initial destination.

47
00:01:47.136 --> 00:01:49.748
Before the transfer starts, review exactly which files will be copied and their total size.

48
00:01:49.748 --> 00:01:53.870
You can change the destination, choose how links are handled, and decide what to do with files that already exist.

49
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:55.088
The copy runs as a background job. Its progress appears below the panes, while browsing stays available.

50
00:01:55.088 --> 00:01:59.728
When you copy the same selection again, Skip Existing keeps files that are already in the backup.

51
00:01:59.728 --> 00:02:02.954
A copied file is written under a temporary name, then renamed into place when complete. An incomplete copy does not replace a finished file.

52
00:02:03.517 --> 00:02:04.064
Create a folder, rename files, and move them to the other pane.

53
00:02:04.064 --> 00:02:06.144
New folder.

54
00:02:06.144 --> 00:02:09.069
Rename. Rename works on one item or on the whole selection.

55
00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:12.353
Move to the other pane. Within one disk a move is a rename; across disks File Commander copies first, then removes.

56
00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:13.471
Delete asks first and is permanent; links go, not what they point to.

57
00:02:13.471 --> 00:02:17.783
Delete is permanent. There is no trash. File Commander lists what it will delete and asks first.

58
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:19.741
Links go, not what they point to. Deleting latest-photos removes the link; photos/2026 stays.

59
00:02:19.741 --> 00:02:21.757
Folders too. A folder is deleted from the inside out, as a background job.

60
00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:22.898
Put the selected names or full paths on the clipboard, in the order you see them.

61
00:02:22.898 --> 00:02:26.010
Copy paths. Copy Paths puts the selected full paths on the clipboard, one per line.

62
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:26.641
Or just the names.

63
00:02:26.641 --> 00:02:29.183
In the order you see. Sort by size first, and the clipboard lists the photos by size.

64
00:02:29.736 --> 00:02:30.296
Search names below a folder and show a result in the pane.

65
00:02:30.296 --> 00:02:33.628
Find files by name. Find searches below the current folder. * and ? work; case is ignored.

66
00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:35.662
Show it in the pane. Show in Pane opens the folder with the cursor on the file.

67
00:02:36.242 --> 00:02:36.791
Browse a zip, copy files out, extract a compressed tar, open any file as an archive, open an encrypted one, and create a zip.

68
00:02:36.791 --> 00:02:39.106
Archives open like folders. Open on a .zip file browses it, read-only.

69
00:02:39.106 --> 00:02:41.322
Copy files out. Copy works as anywhere else.

70
00:02:41.322 --> 00:02:44.981
Read-only. Changing files inside an archive is refused; copy them out first.

71
00:02:44.981 --> 00:02:52.973
Compressed tars ask first. Listing a .tar.gz reads all of it, so Enter offers to extract it in one pass.

72
00:02:52.973 --> 00:02:55.195
Open any file as an archive. A .docx is a zip inside; Open as Archive browses it.

73
00:02:55.195 --> 00:02:59.150
Encrypted archives ask for the password. When a file's data is read; File Commander remembers it until you quit.

74
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:13.800
Make an archive. Copy to a destination named .zip, .7z or .tar.gz, and File Commander creates that archive.

75
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.871
And it opens like a folder.

76
00:03:16.463 --> 00:03:17.011
Jobs run in order while you work; Activity shows them; errors are recorded, not fatal.

77
00:03:17.011 --> 00:03:19.133
Everything runs in the background. Start a copy and keep working. Jobs run one at a time, in order.

78
00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:23.103
Queue another. A second copy waits for the first.

79
00:03:23.103 --> 00:03:24.646
Keep browsing. The panes stay responsive while jobs run.

80
00:03:24.646 --> 00:03:28.145
The Activity window. Every job, its progress and details; pause, resume, cancel or dismiss.

81
00:03:28.145 --> 00:03:30.771
Errors don't stop a job. photos/2025 holds a file nobody can read: the copy records it and goes on.

82
00:03:30.771 --> 00:03:33.554
The Errors window lists them all.

83
00:03:33.554 --> 00:03:34.799
Clear them when you are done.

84
00:03:35.385 --> 00:03:37.529
Limit how fast copies run, and change it while a copy is running.

85
00:03:37.529 --> 00:03:40.768
Change the speed while it runs. Transfer Speed limits copies, moves between disks and S S H transfers.

86
00:03:40.768 --> 00:03:44.795
The copy slows down at once. Even in the middle of a file.

87
00:03:44.795 --> 00:03:47.971
For now or for good. Turn off Keep for future sessions to limit only until you quit.

88
00:03:48.547 --> 00:03:51.811
Pausing holds the whole queue; resume continues; cancel keeps what was copied.

89
00:03:51.811 --> 00:03:53.161
Pause. Pause holds the job between two reads.

90
00:03:53.161 --> 00:04:02.123
Queued jobs wait. Jobs run one at a time, so a paused job holds the queue.

91
00:04:02.123 --> 00:04:03.441
Cancel keeps what is done. Finished files stay; cancelling is not an undo.

92
00:04:03.441 --> 00:04:04.016
The next job starts. The queued copy runs now.

93
00:04:04.553 --> 00:04:05.110
Bookmark folders, servers and archives, and open them from the sidebar.

94
00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:05.697
Bookmark a folder. Bookmark This Folder adds the focused pane’s folder to the sidebar.

95
00:04:05.697 --> 00:04:08.138
Open the sidebar. Show the sidebar to use your bookmarked folders.

96
00:04:08.138 --> 00:04:09.505
Open a bookmark. Click it to open it in the focused pane.

97
00:04:09.505 --> 00:04:12.329
Servers and archives too. Right-click a bookmark to open it in either pane or a new tab, or to remove it.

98
00:04:12.894 --> 00:04:13.450
Connect to a server, browse it, upload a folder, and watch it refresh.

99
00:04:13.451 --> 00:04:24.193
Connect to a server. S S H locations open in a tab. The server needs only Python 3.

100
00:04:24.193 --> 00:04:27.008
A demo server. It runs File Commander's real remote agent on your Mac instead of over the network.

101
00:04:27.008 --> 00:04:30.215
Upload a folder. Copy works between local and remote tabs, and between two servers.

102
00:04:30.215 --> 00:04:33.264
It refreshes by itself. The active S S H tab is checked every three seconds.

103
00:04:33.264 --> 00:04:36.752
Your own servers. Hosts, users, keys and jump hosts come from ~/.ssh/config. Passwords are asked in a sheet.

104
00:04:37.324 --> 00:04:37.877
Fresh shares File Commander’s window and keeps its own tabs and unsaved text.

105
00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:38.756
Open a file in Fresh to replace the file panes with the editor. The file opens as an editor tab.

106
00:04:38.756 --> 00:04:41.202
Edit normally with syntax colors, search, and multiple cursors. File Commander leaves the lifetime of editor tabs to Fresh.

107
00:04:41.202 --> 00:04:41.942
Press F ten to return to the file panes. Switching back keeps the editor session running.

108
00:04:41.942 --> 00:04:44.412
Return to the editor and your unsaved changes are still there. Switching views hides the editor without closing its tabs.

109
00:04:44.959 --> 00:04:45.506
F four turns the window into the editor: Fresh's menus in the menu bar, tabs, the command palette and the file sidebar.

110
00:04:45.506 --> 00:04:47.988
Start a copy first. At 1 mebibytes/s it is still running when the editor opens.

111
00:04:47.989 --> 00:04:50.795
One window. The editor takes the place of the panes; Fresh edits, the window draws its tabs and status bar.

112
00:04:50.795 --> 00:04:53.432
Jobs keep going. The job bar stays below the editor, with its Pause and Cancel buttons.

113
00:04:53.432 --> 00:04:56.465
Fresh's menus. While the editor is shown, the menu bar holds its menus, with Command  shortcuts.

114
00:04:56.465 --> 00:05:02.195
Command palette. Shift Command P finds any editor command by name.

115
00:05:02.195 --> 00:05:07.875
Files beside the text. Command B shows the folder in a sidebar.

116
00:05:07.875 --> 00:05:11.491
Open another file. Without the > the palette finds files of the folder by name.

117
00:05:11.491 --> 00:05:18.218
Files or the editor. The switch in the toolbar goes back and forth, as F ten does; the panes stay as you left them.

118
00:05:18.778 --> 00:05:19.333
Open a file of an S S H tab in the editor; Fresh works on the server and saves there.

119
00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:30.359
Edit on a server. Files in an S S H tab open in the editor too.

120
00:05:30.359 --> 00:05:36.470
Fresh works on the server. Saving, searching and language servers run there; the status bar names it.

121
00:05:36.471 --> 00:05:38.903
Save on the server. Command S writes the file on the server, over the same connection.

122
00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:40.015
Paste photo paths into code, then edit every line at once with multiple cursors.

123
00:05:40.015 --> 00:05:41.532
Copy the photo paths. Select the photos and Copy Paths.

124
00:05:41.532 --> 00:05:42.319
Open the code. app.js builds the photo grid.

125
00:05:42.319 --> 00:05:45.031
Paste them as an array.

126
00:05:45.031 --> 00:05:47.193
Select the common prefix with the mouse.

127
00:05:47.193 --> 00:05:49.979
Every line at once. Ctrl+D adds a cursor at the next match.

128
00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.374
End and Home move every cursor. Quote each name, whatever its length.

129
00:05:54.952 --> 00:05:56.774
After upgrading Fresh, restart its backend; unsaved text survives.

130
00:05:56.774 --> 00:05:58.809
Restart the backend. A running editor keeps the old Fresh until it restarts.

131
00:05:58.809 --> 00:06:01.462
Your text is still there. Fresh saved its recovery data before stopping.

132
00:06:02.019 --> 00:06:02.583
Settings › Editor can show Fresh's own terminal interface in place of the native editor.

133
00:06:02.583 --> 00:06:07.472
Fresh's own look. Settings › Editor › Show the editor as a terminal runs its terminal interface instead.

134
00:06:07.473 --> 00:06:09.561
Same place, same Fresh. It takes the panes' place too, with the same tabs and unsaved text.

135
00:06:09.561 --> 00:06:12.188
Its own keys. Typing, F-keys and Control keys go to Fresh, ahead of File Commander's menus.

136
00:06:12.188 --> 00:06:13.540
F ten comes back. Fresh's own switch key detaches it, and the panes return.

137
00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:14.704
Read and edit Markdown with proportional text, larger headings, images and tables, using the available pane width.

138
00:06:14.704 --> 00:06:15.502
Start with Markdown. One file can be read as source or edited as a composed page.

139
00:06:15.502 --> 00:06:17.716
Open Compose. The editor palette contains Markdown: Toggle Compose/Preview.

140
00:06:17.716 --> 00:06:20.115
Room for the page. Proportional text and larger headings use the available pane width.

141
00:06:20.116 --> 00:06:21.250
Edit in place. Typing changes the Markdown document while the composed view stays visible.

142
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.067
Images and tables. Images and measured table cells are part of the document flow.

143
00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:24.807
Back to the files. The editor keeps this document and its unsaved changes.

144
00:06:25.374 --> 00:06:25.948
Find any command in the palette, rebind its key, and pick a key preset.

145
00:06:25.948 --> 00:06:28.894
Every command, one search away. The command palette finds commands by any part of their name.

146
00:06:28.894 --> 00:06:30.457
Change its key. Select a command, press Control K, then the new key.

147
00:06:30.457 --> 00:06:32.648
The new key works.

148
00:06:32.648 --> 00:06:36.475
Two presets. Commander keeps the terminal keys; Mac uses Finder’s shortcuts. Settings › Keys lists every key.

149
00:06:37.016 --> 00:06:37.557
Light and dark, colors for both, and a font and size for every element.

150
00:06:37.557 --> 00:06:41.533
Light or dark. Follow the system, or choose one in Settings › General.

151
00:06:41.533 --> 00:06:43.937
The theme editor. Every element has a color for light and for dark, and a font and size.

152
00:06:43.937 --> 00:06:49.092
Bigger names in the list. Changes show at once, in the preview and in the window.

153
00:06:49.092 --> 00:06:52.883
Colors. Pick a color for light and dark, or a system color that follows your accent color.

154
00:06:52.883 --> 00:06:55.109
Save, revert or reset. Save keeps it as your own theme; Revert drops the edits.

155
00:06:55.676 --> 00:06:56.228
Automate File Commander with Loo ah: a script copies the camera's photos.

156
00:06:56.228 --> 00:06:57.996
Scripts drive File Commander. A Loo ah script runs commands, presses keys and waits for jobs.

157
00:06:57.996 --> 00:07:00.725
Run Loo ah Script. Runs it with fc run, in a terminal window. This one copies every photo to the backup.

158
00:07:00.725 --> 00:07:03.752
The scripting guide. doc/scripting.md describes the API, events and the state table.
